1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680
 Hey everyone, it's Dr. Marcon and this is chapter 10 on skeletal muscle tissue.

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:08,520
 So what is muscle?

3
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:12,960
 Muscle, it's derived from the Latin word for little mouse.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:18,760
 We know that muscle is the primary tissue in the heart as well as the walls of hollow

5
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:25,080
 organs and we also have skeletal muscle tissue which makes up nearly half the body's mass.

6
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:29,960
 So with the heart we have our cardiac muscle tissue within the walls of hollow organs.

7
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:34,240
 We have smooth muscle tissue.

8
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:39,200
 We have different properties of muscle tissue, we have contractility.

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:44,620
 So myofilaments, these are filaments within the muscle, are responsible for shortening

10
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:51,780
 of muscle cells and we have two types of myofilaments, we have actin and myosin.

11
00:00:51,780 --> 00:00:57,960
 Excitability, with this property nerve cells excite muscle cells causing electrical impulses

12
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:02,760
 to travel along the sarcolema.

13
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:09,220
 Extensibility, so contraction of a skeletal muscle stretches the opposing muscle.

14
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:13,760
 We also know that smooth muscle is stretched by substances within that hollow organ.

15
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:19,140
 For example, when we have food in our stomach this stretches the smooth muscle in the stomach

16
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:22,480
 as well as urine in the urinary bladder.

17
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:26,400
 The last property we'll talk about is elasticity.

18
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:29,640
 Muscle has the ability to recoil after being stretched.

19
00:01:29,640 --> 00:01:33,720
 We talked about the different connective fibers or connective tissue fibers that are

20
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:42,440
 within muscle such as for example elastic tissue allowing for that recoil property.

21
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:46,600
 So just want to go over the terminology that is specific to muscle tissue.

22
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:53,560
 Whenever you see the prefixes myo or myes, this prefix means muscle, sarco.

23
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:58,200
 This is also another prefix pertaining to muscle tissue, sarco is a prefix meaning flesh.

24
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:04,760
 So for example, we have specific organelles for muscle tissue that relate to generalized

25
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:06,080
 organelles of cells.

26
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:12,920
 So with the sarcolema, the sarcolema is the plasma membrane of muscle cells.

27
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:21,160
 Sarcoplasm is the cytoplasm of muscle cells.

28
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,200
 We have different functions of muscle tissue.

29
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,240
 One of the main functions of course is to produce movement.

30
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:34,600
 Skeletal muscle is attached to skeletal bone and moves the body by moving the bones.

31
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,600
 Smooth muscle.

32
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:36,600
 Smooth muscle is important.

33
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:39,720
 We know we find them in the linings of the hollow organs.

34
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:46,560
 Smooth muscle squeezes fluids and other substances like a baby out of the uterus through the hollow

35
00:02:46,560 --> 00:02:51,440
 organs.

36
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:57,240
 Smooth muscle tissue is also important because we can find them in the opening and closing

37
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:58,840
 of body passageways.

38
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:02,000
 We have muscles called sphincters.

39
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,160
 So these sphincters open and close body passageways.

40
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:07,840
 They basically function as vowels.

41
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:16,400
 They can open to allow passage of a substance and then contract to close the passageway.

42
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:21,100
 Muscle tissue also helps maintain posture and stabilize joints.

43
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:24,420
 This enables the body to remain sitting or standing.

44
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:28,960
 Also muscle tone helps stabilize many synovial joints.

45
00:03:28,960 --> 00:03:31,480
 Muscle tissue is also important for heat generation.

46
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:37,000
 So muscle contractions help produce heat and helps with the homeostatic nature of the body

47
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,440
 helps maintain normal body temperature.

48
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:46,600
 So in the first unit, we talked about the different types of muscle tissue.

49
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:49,080
 We know there are three types of muscle tissue.

50
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:54,120
 We have skeletal muscle tissue, cardiac muscle tissue, and smooth muscle tissue.

51
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:58,720
 So in this chapter, we're going to talk about these different types of muscle tissue.

52
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:04,000
 We know what they look like under a microscope, but let's talk a little bit about each type

53
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 of muscle tissue.

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,520
 So first, we'll talk about skeletal muscle tissue.

55
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:16,200
 Skeletal muscle tissue is packaged into skeletal muscles and makes up about 40% of our body

56
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:17,200
 weight.

57
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:22,480
 We know that the cells of skeletal muscle tissue are striated, meaning they have those

58
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:25,880
 alternating dark and light bands.

59
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:30,000
 We know that skeletal muscle is innervated by voluntary division of the nervous system,

60
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,960
 meaning we do have voluntary control over our skeletal muscle.

61
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:40,200
 The second type of muscle tissue is cardiac muscle tissue.

62
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:43,880
 We know that this is found only in the walls of the heart.

63
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:50,160
 Again, we'll see lovely striations of cardiac muscle tissue, however, unlike skeletal muscle,

64
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:57,280
 the cardiac muscle cells are branching, they're uni-nucleates, and they have structures where

65
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:01,240
 these cells interdigitate, and these are called intercalated discs.

66
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:05,560
 This kind of separates with regards to what they look like.

67
00:05:05,560 --> 00:05:09,380
 It kind of differentiates cardiac muscle from skeletal muscle.

68
00:05:09,380 --> 00:05:15,280
 Also one of the things that kind of differentiate cardiac muscle from skeletal muscle is that

69
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:18,280
 cardiac muscle contraction is involuntary.

70
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:23,560
 Again, we're not sitting here telling our heart to beat as we sit there.

71
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:27,800
 No, the cardiac muscle actually, there are cardiac centers within our brain.

72
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:36,280
 Also the cells within the heart are under, they have their own ability to contract on

73
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:37,280
 their own.

74
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:41,080
 Then the third type of muscle tissue is smooth muscle tissue.

75
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:46,680
 We know that this is located in the walls of hollow organs just by the name alone.

76
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:52,800
 We know that these cells do not have striations, and again, they are innervated by involuntary

77
00:05:52,800 --> 00:05:59,760
 division of the nervous system, meaning they are under involuntary control.

78
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:06,060
 So with skeletal muscle, each muscle is an organ, consists mostly of muscle tissue.

79
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:14,880
 Skeletal muscle also contains connective tissue as well as blood vessels and nerves.

80
00:06:14,880 --> 00:06:19,680
 Going over the gross anatomy of a skeletal muscle, it is made up of connective tissue

81
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,880
 and fascicles.

82
00:06:21,880 --> 00:06:27,920
 We have shes of connective tissue that bind a skeletal muscle and is fibrous together.

83
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:36,920
 So the outermost covering or outermost sheath is known as the epimesium.

84
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:43,440
 Epimesium is dense regular connective tissue surrounding the entire muscle.

85
00:06:43,440 --> 00:06:45,880
 The middle layer is the paramecium.

86
00:06:45,880 --> 00:06:51,560
 So paramecium surrounds each fascicle, and we know that a fascicle is a group of muscle

87
00:06:51,560 --> 00:06:53,080
 fibers.

88
00:06:53,080 --> 00:06:59,680
 And surrounding each individual muscle fiber is the innermost layer, which is the endomesium.

89
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:05,280
 Endomesium is a fine sheath of connective tissue, wrapping each muscle cell or muscle

90
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:06,280
 fiber.

91
00:07:06,280 --> 00:07:13,720
 So we have muscle fibers or individual muscle cells that come together and form a fascicle.

92
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:17,440
 Now we know that the individual muscle cells surrounded by endomesium.

93
00:07:17,440 --> 00:07:25,320
 And then the groups of muscle fibers that form the fascicle is covered by paramecium.

94
00:07:25,320 --> 00:07:29,840
 And a bunch of muscle fascicles make up an entire muscle.

95
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:37,840
 And this is surrounded by sheaths of connective tissue known as the epimesium, which is the

96
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:44,640
 outermost layer of connective tissue.

97
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:51,480
 So these connective tissue sheaths that surround the entire muscle or the epimesium is continuous

98
00:07:51,480 --> 00:07:53,700
 with tendons.

99
00:07:53,700 --> 00:08:01,080
 So when muscle fibers contract, a pull is exerted on all layers of connective tissue.

100
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:03,520
 And this forms a tendon.

101
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:13,280
 Sheaths provide elasticity and also carry blood vessels and nerves.

102
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:19,680
 So here we see a diagram of the gross anatomy of the skeletal muscle along with its connective

103
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,600
 tissue layer sheaths.

104
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:30,780
 So we know that an individual muscle fiber or muscle cell is surrounded by endomesium.

105
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:32,920
 That's the innermost lining.

106
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:41,000
 All these individual muscle fibers form a muscle fascicle.

107
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,820
 So the fascicle is surrounded by paramecium.

108
00:08:45,820 --> 00:08:52,240
 So we see all these muscle fibers forming a fascicle.

109
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,880
 This fascicle is surrounded by paramecium.

110
00:08:55,880 --> 00:09:01,200
 And then a bunch of fascicles will then form the actual skeletal muscle.

111
00:09:01,200 --> 00:09:07,880
 And the covering for the entire muscle is epimesium.

112
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,240
 This is the outermost layer.

113
00:09:10,240 --> 00:09:23,280
 And we know that epimesium and the connective tissue sheaths are continuous with a tendon.

114
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:28,920
 So each skeletal muscle is, with regards to nerves supply and blood supply, each skeletal

115
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:36,000
 muscle is supplied by branches of one nerve, one artery, and then one or more veins.

116
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:41,640
 Mirrors and vessels will branch repeatedly, and the smallest branches will serve individual

117
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:46,720
 muscle fibers or individual muscle cells.

118
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:49,640
 Now how are these muscles attached?

119
00:09:49,640 --> 00:09:55,200
 So with regards to muscle attachments, most skeletal muscles run from one bone to another.

120
00:09:55,200 --> 00:10:01,280
 So one bone will move while the other bone remains fixed.

121
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:07,240
 So the less movable attachment, this is known as the muscle's origin.

122
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:13,120
 Okay, so this is the less movable attachment of the skeletal muscle.

123
00:10:13,120 --> 00:10:18,280
 The insertion of the skeletal muscle is the more movable attachment.

124
00:10:18,280 --> 00:10:26,160
 So the skeletal muscle will insert onto a bone, and this bone is more movable, meaning it moves

125
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:28,720
 more than the origin.

126
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:34,400
 So the origin is the less movable attachment, the insertion of a skeletal muscle is the

127
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:38,520
 more movable attachment.

128
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,600
 Here we have an example of the brachialis muscle.

129
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:47,480
 We know that the origin of the brachialis muscle is along the shaft of the humerus.

130
00:10:47,480 --> 00:11:00,680
 It will insert into the ulna, the ulna here we see is more medial, it has its attachments

131
00:11:00,680 --> 00:11:08,120
 on the coronoid process, which is the proximal portion of the ulna.

132
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:16,320
 So because the humerus is the less movable attachment, this is known as the brachialis's

133
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:23,920
 origin, and the muscle or the muscle tenon will then attach to the proximal portion of

134
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:26,800
 the ulna or the coronoid process of the ulna.

135
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:30,880
 And we know that the ulna is the bone that actually ends up moving the most.

136
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:36,680
 So this is known as the insertion, this is by indirect attachment.

137
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:43,000
 So the brachialis has its origin on the humerus, the shaft of the humerus, and its insertion

138
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:50,480
 on the proximal portion of the ulna, and we know we can move the forearm closer to the

139
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:51,480
 humerus.

140
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:57,480
 So decreasing this angle between the ulna and the radius and the humerus, this is known

141
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:59,840
 as flexion.

142
00:11:59,840 --> 00:12:08,160
 So decreasing this angle is flexion, and then increasing the angle between the two structures

143
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:19,200
 is known as extension, like extending your arm.

144
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:25,120
 So with regards to muscle attachments, muscle attached to origins and assertions by connective

145
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:33,680
 tissue, with the fleshy attachments, the connective tissues, fibers are short, indirect attachments,

146
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:38,600
 connective tissue forms, a tendon or aponeurosis.

147
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:44,680
 Bone markings present where tendons meet bones, I'm sorry, bone markings are present where

148
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:45,680
 tendons meet bones.

149
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:50,560
 So again, we talked about the different bony markings that can serve as attachment sites

150
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:52,120
 for either tendons or muscles.

151
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:59,560
 So these include tubercles, trochanters, and crests.

152
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:06,160
 So then we're going to talk about muscle fibers, the microscopic and functional anatomy of

153
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:09,640
 skeletal muscle tissue specifically, the skeletal muscle fiber.

154
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:13,040
 We know that the muscle fiber is also known as the muscle cell.

155
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:16,880
 The skeletal muscle fiber is the basic unit of skeletal muscle.

156
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:19,200
 These fibers are long and cylindrical.

157
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:21,680
 They are huge cells.

158
00:13:21,680 --> 00:13:26,980
 Diameter of these cells are anywhere between 10 to 100 micrometers or microns.

159
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:34,560
 The length of these muscle fibers can go from several centimeters to dozens of centimeters.

160
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:37,320
 Each cell is formed by fusion of embryonic cells.

161
00:13:37,320 --> 00:13:42,680
 We know that these cells are multi-nucleate, meaning they have many nuclei, and the nuclei

162
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:46,680
 are peripherally located.

163
00:13:46,680 --> 00:13:52,200
 So here we see the microscopic anatomy of skeletal muscle fiber, and again, skeletal

164
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:56,240
 muscle fiber is also known as the muscle cell.

165
00:13:56,240 --> 00:14:07,120
 So we have individual myofibrils that are basically just long rods within cytoplasm.

166
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:15,960
 So striations of the skeletal muscle are a result of the structure of these myofibrils.

167
00:14:15,960 --> 00:14:24,040
 So we have myofibrils that make up the skeletal muscle cell or skeletal muscle fiber.

168
00:14:24,040 --> 00:14:30,640
 And again, we have the sheath of connective tissues surrounding individual muscle fibers

169
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:33,640
 known as endo-museum.

170
00:14:33,640 --> 00:14:41,680
 So this individual muscle fiber, we have the different organelles that make up this cell.

171
00:14:41,680 --> 00:14:42,920
 We have the nuclei.

172
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:43,920
 We have the mitochondria.

173
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:50,720
 Again, there are lots of mitochondria in skeletal muscle fibers because they need more energy.

174
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:57,440
 And then we see the alternating striations of dark bands and light bands, dark bands.

175
00:14:57,440 --> 00:15:01,560
 You know, there's the letter A in dark, so it represents an A band.

176
00:15:01,560 --> 00:15:04,440
 And the light band has an I in it, so this represents an I band.

177
00:15:04,440 --> 00:15:10,040
 And we'll talk about the different bands when we talk about the functional unit of the skeletal

178
00:15:10,040 --> 00:15:13,840
 muscle tissue, which is the sarcomere in just a little bit.

179
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:20,120
 So here we see just a diagram of one individual muscle fiber, again made up of myofibrils and

180
00:15:20,120 --> 00:15:25,720
 other organelles.

181
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:32,680
 So myofibrils, the striations of the skeletal muscle fiber is due to the internal structure

182
00:15:32,680 --> 00:15:34,920
 of myofibrils.

183
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:41,280
 Myofibrils are long rods within the cytoplasm, make up about 80% of the cytoplasm, and are

184
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:45,840
 a specialized contractile organell found in muscle tissue.

185
00:15:45,840 --> 00:15:52,400
 And these myofibrils are made up of long rows of repeating segments called sarcomeres.

186
00:15:52,400 --> 00:16:00,240
 And the sarcomeres are the functional unit of skeletal muscle tissue.

187
00:16:00,240 --> 00:16:05,280
 So a sarcomere, again, being the basic unit of contraction of skeletal muscle, has different

188
00:16:05,280 --> 00:16:06,480
 elements to it.

189
00:16:06,480 --> 00:16:10,040
 We have what's known as Z disks or the Z line.

190
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,320
 These are the boundaries of each sarcomere.

191
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:18,320
 It is then made up of different filaments, we have thin actin filaments that will extend

192
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:21,360
 from the Z disks toward the center of the sarcomere.

193
00:16:21,360 --> 00:16:24,040
 And then we have our thick myosin filaments.

194
00:16:24,040 --> 00:16:28,240
 These are located in the center of the sarcomere.

195
00:16:28,240 --> 00:16:36,440
 And they, the ends overlap, the inner ends overlap with the ends of the thin filaments.

196
00:16:36,440 --> 00:16:43,920
 And the thick myosin filaments contain ATPase enzymes, which is important for breaking down

197
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:51,200
 ATP into its components to release energy.

198
00:16:51,200 --> 00:16:58,120
 The A bands, which are made up of the dark bands, make up the full length of the thick

199
00:16:58,120 --> 00:17:03,720
 myosin filaments, and will also include the inner ends of the thin filaments.

200
00:17:03,720 --> 00:17:10,160
 The H zone is the center part of the A band, where no thin filaments occur, so it's made

201
00:17:10,160 --> 00:17:13,080
 up just of the thick myosin filaments.

202
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:19,680
 A bands and I bands reflect polarized light differently, so A bands are known as anisotropic

203
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:24,800
 or isi bands are isotropic.

204
00:17:24,800 --> 00:17:30,680
 Now the M line, the M line is in the center of that H zone.

205
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:35,480
 So the M line contains tiny rods that will hold thick filaments together.

206
00:17:35,480 --> 00:17:39,800
 And then the I band, that light band is the region where with only thin filaments, meaning

207
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:44,960
 there are no thick filaments within this region, and lies within two adjacent sarcomeres.

208
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:51,080
 So if we look at a picture of our sarcomere, we can see that we have the Z disks.

209
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:57,520
 So from Z disks to Z disks makes up one sarcomere, okay, so Z disks to Z disks.

210
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:05,120
 From the Z disk, we have the thin filaments that extend from this line or the Z line,

211
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:14,360
 and then they will extend and then overlap with the dark myosin thick filaments here.

212
00:18:14,360 --> 00:18:19,760
 So these darker red lines, those are the thick myosin filaments, and you can see they can

213
00:18:19,760 --> 00:18:25,160
 overlap with the center actin filaments.

214
00:18:25,160 --> 00:18:33,000
 And the H zone is where only thick filaments occur, and within the H zone you have that

215
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:38,800
 M line that hold the thick filaments together.

216
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:45,800
 So the A band will go from the end of thick myosin filaments to another.

217
00:18:45,800 --> 00:18:51,360
 It will contain areas where they overlap with thin filaments, however you reach the middle

218
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:54,200
 part which is the H zone where there are no thin filaments.

219
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:58,920
 And we can actually see this a little bit better in the next slide.

220
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:01,120
 So here we see one sarcomere.

221
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:03,640
 So here's a Z disk, here's the Z disk.

222
00:19:03,640 --> 00:19:09,360
 So from Z disk to Z disk, this is one sarcomere, okay.

223
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:16,480
 And then extending from the Z line is a thin filament, this line in blue.

224
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:18,760
 So this is a thin filament.

225
00:19:18,760 --> 00:19:24,560
 And we can see at some point that part of the thin filament will overlap with the thick

226
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:27,820
 myosin filament, okay.

227
00:19:27,820 --> 00:19:35,600
 And then from the end of one thick filament to another, that makes the A band, which is

228
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:37,400
 the dark band.

229
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:41,960
 So let's actually draw this out, which I love to do.

230
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:54,720
 Okay, so from here to here, that's the A band that is made up of both thick and thin filaments,

231
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:58,600
 which makes it dark.

232
00:19:58,600 --> 00:20:05,000
 And then we'll have a part of the A band where there are no thin filaments, so we can see

233
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,240
 the thin filament kind of ends here.

234
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:18,920
 So this zone right here, this is known as the H band, or the H region.

235
00:20:18,920 --> 00:20:27,720
 So there are no thin filaments here, it's just dark myosin, or thick myosin filaments.

236
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:33,080
 And then in the center of this H band, we have the M line, and this is where the thick

237
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:36,880
 myosin filaments will attach, okay.

238
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:42,720
 So make sure you know the different structures of the sarcomere, what makes up the A band,

239
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:47,840
 what is within the H band, what is the M line, okay.

240
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:59,720
 So make sure to study that.

241
00:20:59,720 --> 00:21:01,960
 So there are other structures within the sarcomere.

242
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:07,120
 We have titan, titan is a spring like molecule that will resist over stretching.

243
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:11,920
 Titan molecules will extend from the z-dist to thick filaments to the M line.

244
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:16,080
 Two functions of titan includes the whole of the thick filaments in place, and then unfolds

245
00:21:16,080 --> 00:21:21,120
 when the muscle is stretched.

246
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:28,840
 So the sarco-plasmic reticulum is a specialized type of smooth endoplasmic reticulum.

247
00:21:28,840 --> 00:21:33,600
 It's just basically the organelle, the same organelle within a generalized cell, but in

248
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:35,880
 muscle cells.

249
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:40,440
 So there are interconnecting tubules that surround each myofibril.

250
00:21:40,440 --> 00:21:45,000
 Some tubules will form cross chimes called terminal cisterns.

251
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:49,920
 Cyterns occur in pairs on either side of a T tubule.

252
00:21:49,920 --> 00:21:56,160
 So pairs of cisterns plus the T tubule actually makes up a triad.

253
00:21:56,160 --> 00:22:00,920
 The sarco-plasmic reticulum contains calcium ions.

254
00:22:00,920 --> 00:22:02,440
 Calcium ions are important.

255
00:22:02,440 --> 00:22:08,400
 It's an important ion because they are released when muscle is simulated to contract.

256
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:13,240
 Calcium ions will then diffuse through the cytoplasm and help trigger the sliding filament

257
00:22:13,240 --> 00:22:16,760
 mechanism, the video in which I asked you guys to watch.

258
00:22:16,760 --> 00:22:24,040
 So basically calcium will bind to sites on the thin actin filaments to allow the thick

259
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:31,800
 myosin filaments to bind to the sites on the actin filament and then allow for contraction

260
00:22:31,800 --> 00:22:39,160
 by movement of the thin filaments towards the center of the sarcomere.

261
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:45,080
 Now T tubules, these are deep invaginations of the sarcolema.

262
00:22:45,080 --> 00:22:50,920
 And the triad again is that T tubule are flanked by two terminal cisterns and this structure

263
00:22:50,920 --> 00:22:57,220
 is important because when we have the electrical signal coming from the nervous system for

264
00:22:57,220 --> 00:23:04,720
 our muscle to contract, the electrical signal will then travel down the T tubules and then

265
00:23:04,720 --> 00:23:11,080
 allow for basically the stimulation to release calcium ions.

266
00:23:11,080 --> 00:23:15,240
 And then this will allow for contraction of the muscle.

267
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:21,840
 So here we see the sarcoplasmic reticulum and T tubules in the skeletal muscle fiber.

268
00:23:21,840 --> 00:23:31,040
 So this white band right here, this is the T tubule and then surrounded by a pair of cisterns.

269
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:33,520
 Those are the, that makes up the triad.

270
00:23:33,520 --> 00:23:38,880
 So once we have again a signal for contraction to occur, this signal will travel down the

271
00:23:38,880 --> 00:23:45,020
 T tubules to then go along the tubules of the sarcoplasmic reticulum to allow for the

272
00:23:45,020 --> 00:23:52,040
 release of calcium ions and then allowing for contraction to occur.

273
00:23:52,040 --> 00:23:55,320
 There are two types, two major types of contraction.

274
00:23:55,320 --> 00:23:57,120
 We have concentric contraction.

275
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:01,920
 This is when muscle will shorten to do work and then we have eccentric contraction.

276
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:05,720
 Basically the muscle will generate force as it lengthens.

277
00:24:05,720 --> 00:24:11,520
 This muscle acts sort of as a break to help resist gravity.

278
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:15,440
 This is, for example, the down portion of a pushup.

279
00:24:15,440 --> 00:24:21,320
 So that is an example of eccentric contraction.

280
00:24:21,320 --> 00:24:23,960
 So this brings us to the mechanism of contraction.

281
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:29,520
 We have what's known as the sliding filament mechanism theory, which explains concentric

282
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:31,200
 contraction.

283
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:38,600
 So we have myosin heads, myosin heads contained or located on the thick filaments.

284
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:44,000
 These myosin heads will attach to thin filaments, receptors on the thin filaments at both ends

285
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,600
 of a sarcomere.

286
00:24:46,600 --> 00:24:52,920
 The myosin heads on the thick filament will then pull the thin filaments towards the center

287
00:24:52,920 --> 00:24:54,900
 of the sarcomere.

288
00:24:54,900 --> 00:25:00,240
 Now while this is happening, the sarcomere does shorten, however the thin and thick filaments

289
00:25:00,240 --> 00:25:01,240
 do not.

290
00:25:01,240 --> 00:25:06,600
 They just slide past one another and don't actually shorten, they just move.

291
00:25:06,600 --> 00:25:11,420
 So thin and thick filaments do not shorten, but the sarcomere length will.

292
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:13,880
 This is what causes contraction.

293
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:19,480
 So this whole mechanism is initiated by the release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic

294
00:25:19,480 --> 00:25:20,880
 reticulum.

295
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:27,200
 And of course, this is an action that does require energy, so this mechanism is powered

296
00:25:27,200 --> 00:25:32,720
 by ATP.

297
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:38,120
 So here we kind of see a close look at the different filaments that make up the sarcomere.

298
00:25:38,120 --> 00:25:45,300
 So we have the thin actin filaments that have receptors for these kind of golf club leg

299
00:25:45,300 --> 00:25:46,300
 structures.

300
00:25:46,300 --> 00:25:49,680
 These are the myosin heads of the thick myosin filaments.

301
00:25:49,680 --> 00:25:57,360
 So once calcium enters the scene with the release of an electrical stimulation from the

302
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:02,680
 nervous system, basically what happens is calcium will help.

303
00:26:02,680 --> 00:26:09,520
 Help open the receptor sites for the myosin heads on the thin filaments.

304
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:17,840
 And using ATP, these myosin heads will then kind of pull the thin filament towards the

305
00:26:17,840 --> 00:26:24,200
 center of the sarcomere, allowing for the two filaments to slide past one another, causing

306
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:25,200
 a contraction.

307
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:30,320
 So the myosin heads attach to act into the thin filaments, then pivot to pull the thin

308
00:26:30,320 --> 00:26:36,600
 filaments inwards or towards the center of the sarcomere.

309
00:26:36,600 --> 00:26:43,260
 So the contraction of these filaments or the sarcomere changes the striation pattern.

310
00:26:43,260 --> 00:26:49,840
 When fully relaxed, the thin filaments will partially overlap the thick filaments.

311
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:55,680
 And then contraction occurs as the Z disks move closer together.

312
00:26:55,680 --> 00:27:00,800
 The sarcomere length shortens, the I band will shorten, and the H zone.

313
00:27:00,800 --> 00:27:08,720
 So the H zone where there were no thin filaments will actually disappear.

314
00:27:08,720 --> 00:27:12,120
 However, the A band remains the same length.

315
00:27:12,120 --> 00:27:17,480
 So the A band being from the end of the thick filament to the other.

316
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:19,960
 So A band remains the same length.

317
00:27:19,960 --> 00:27:20,960
 Okay.

318
00:27:20,960 --> 00:27:24,640
 So make sure you study this slide and kind of understand what happens to the different

319
00:27:24,640 --> 00:27:34,000
 bands during contraction, which one shortens, which one stays the same, that kind of thing.

